<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyễn Du</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen Du</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nguyễn Du was a Vietnamese poet and official, regarded as the greatest poet in the Vietnamese language. After serving the Lê court, he took office under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a> and led an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1813. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-kieu">Truyện Kiều</a>, or The Tale of Kieu, a verse novel of more than three thousand lines written in chữ Nôm, is the most celebrated work of Vietnamese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-du.jpg" alt="Nguyễn Du" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vienvo03 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_%C4%91%C3%A0i_c%E1%BB%A5_Nguy%E1%BB%85n_Du.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nguyễn Du was born on 3 January 1766 in the Bích Câu area just outside the Imperial Citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, near the Temple of Literature. His father, Nguyễn Nghiễm, was a high-ranked minister, and the family came from the village of Tiên Điền in what is now Hà Tĩnh Province. Many of its members passed the civil service examinations and became poets. His father retired in 1771 and died in 1776, and his mother died in 1778, leaving him an orphan at twelve.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> rebels swept north and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-chieu-thong">Lê Chiêu Thống</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, Du took refuge in his wife's homeland of Quỳnh Côi. He later called the years that followed ten years of wind and dust. In 1796 he set out to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a> in the south but was arrested by a Tây Sơn general and held in Nghệ An for three months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> and became Emperor Gia Long. Du pledged his support when the emperor's procession passed Nghệ An and was made district magistrate of Phù Dung, then prefectural magistrate of Thường Tín. In late 1807 he presided over the provincial examinations in Hải Dương, and in 1809 he became Administrative Commissioner of Quảng Bình. Sources differ on his conduct: his family's genealogy calls him simple and loved, while the official biographies describe him as frequently humiliated by superiors and nervous before the emperor.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a> died in February 1820 and his son Minh Mạng asked Du to lead a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> to announce the change of ruler. Du fell ill, probably with cholera, and died on 16 September 1820 at the age of 54. Minh Mạng gave him the posthumous name Trung Thanh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Du wrote in Literary Chinese and in the Nôm script, in forms ranging from Vietnamese adaptations of Tang styles to the folk metres song thất lục bát and lục bát. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-kieu">Truyện Kiều</a>, originally titled Đoạn trường tân thanh, is a verse novel of 3,254 lines in lục bát. It prefers the vernacular to learned Sino-Vietnamese vocabulary and introduced idioms that remain in common use. Scholars disagree on when it was written. Popular belief places it after his trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1813, but other evidence suggests a date before 1809, or even before the Nguyễn dynasty began in 1802.</p><p>In the early twentieth century Du became a national figure. In 1924 the critic Phạm Quỳnh praised the poem as the guardian of the language, and a debate followed in which Ngô Đức Kế and Huỳnh Thúc Kháng objected to honouring a poem about sex work at a time of colonial rule. His ancestral house in Hà Tĩnh was designated a Special National Site in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nguyễn Du” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Literature flourished in both Chinese characters and the vernacular chu Nom script: <b>Nguyen Du</b>, an official who served as envoy to China, composed The Tale of Kieu, the most celebrated work of Vietnamese poetry, in the early nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hà Tĩnh, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.33,105.9&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Hà Tĩnh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nguyễn Du"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3867 5921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3867 5921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.33,105.9&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
